WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.167 --> 00:00:02.002
-Hello everyone.

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:06.340
I want to welcome you
to this CIFAR virtual talk.

00:00:06.924 --> 00:00:09.384
It's part of a new series
of events

00:00:09.384 --> 00:00:11.595
meant to shed light on
how research

00:00:12.054 --> 00:00:14.640
can enlighten
decision-making.

00:00:15.057 --> 00:00:17.643
The research in health,
of course, in public health.

00:00:19.102 --> 00:00:22.981
But also in computing
and in many other fields

00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:24.149
of human and social sciences,

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:27.402
in economy, in anthropology,
in geography.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:31.114
How this research can
then help the decision makers

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:32.616
to make their decision,

00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:36.078
at a key moment
where the governments begin

00:00:36.537 --> 00:00:38.830
to open their economies
that were paralyzed,

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.458
as we know,
by COVID-19.

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:44.378
So, if you don't know CIFAR,

00:00:44.378 --> 00:00:47.756
it's a global charity
organization

00:00:47.756 --> 00:00:50.384
that is established in Canada
and that gathers the most

00:00:50.384 --> 00:00:53.011
brilliant brains of the world

00:00:53.011 --> 00:00:55.973
to mull over the important
questions

00:00:55.973 --> 00:00:58.767
that humanity and sciences
are facing.

00:00:59.518 --> 00:01:01.186
And, like many organizations
around the globe,

00:01:01.186 --> 00:01:03.564
CIFAR, obviously, had to adapt
quickly to fight

00:01:03.564 --> 00:01:07.025
against COVID-19
and throw new projects
and initiatives

00:01:07.025 --> 00:01:09.861
that we hope to shed the light
during this series.

00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:12.406
So, as for me,
I am Antoine Petit.

00:01:12.406 --> 00:01:13.657
You've understood
by my accent

00:01:13.657 --> 00:01:14.992
that I am French.

00:01:14.992 --> 00:01:17.286
I am a computing professor

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:20.247
but I am also
and maybe especially,
at this moment,

00:01:20.247 --> 00:01:22.332
chief executive officer of the
CNRS, which is

00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:26.295
the biggest French national
center of, multidisciplinary
research 

00:01:26.295 --> 00:01:29.006
a public research organization.

00:01:29.464 --> 00:01:33.093
But I am also a
member of the research council
of the CIFAR,

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:35.804
and which the mandate
is to advise the president

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:39.141
and the chief executive
on the formulation, the
creation,

00:01:39.141 --> 00:01:41.935
and the establishment of
advanced research of outstanding
quality programs

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:45.147
as well as the closure
of these programs

00:01:45.147 --> 00:01:47.024
once their mission
is accomplished.

00:01:47.524 --> 00:01:49.860
And I have the great pleasure
of presenting you

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.154
Professor Yoshua Bengio.

00:01:52.529 --> 00:01:57.034
Yoshua is the chair holder
in artificial
intelligence Canada-CIFAR,

00:01:57.034 --> 00:01:58.118
in the Mila center.

00:01:58.577 --> 00:02:01.705
He is also co-director
of the automatic learning
program,

00:02:01.705 --> 00:02:03.749
biological learning of
CIFAR,

00:02:04.041 --> 00:02:06.126
and professor at the Université
de Montréal.

00:02:06.877 --> 00:02:09.713
He's part of the most quoted
and respected

00:02:09.713 --> 00:02:11.089
computer scientists in the
world.

00:02:11.465 --> 00:02:13.759
He's one of the pioneers
of profound learning

00:02:14.176 --> 00:02:18.764
and he is mainly the winner
of the prestigious Turing prize
in 2018

00:02:18.764 --> 00:02:21.391
with his two colleagues
Geoffrey Hinton and Yann Le Cun.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.269
And the Turing prize is
considered, in a certain way,

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:26.271
the Nobel prize of computing

00:02:27.022 --> 00:02:29.691
and that award
is absolutely exceptional.

00:02:30.942 --> 00:02:33.570
And Yoshua, he will tell us a
bit about it later,

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.405
also directed the development of
COVI

00:02:35.405 --> 00:02:37.616
the application for smartphones,

00:02:39.076 --> 00:02:41.203
which aims to look for contacts

00:02:41.203 --> 00:02:44.414
and to predict the risks
of infection to fight against
COVID-19.

00:02:45.248 --> 00:02:48.210
Before we start, I want to
remind you that today's session

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.836
will last about 30 minutes.

00:02:50.379 --> 00:02:52.547
And I'm going to take advantage
of my role as session chairman

00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:55.509
to start asking Joshua
some first questions,

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:58.804
but you will also be able to
contribute to these questions

00:02:58.804 --> 00:03:03.475
by using the function question
answer.

00:03:06.228 --> 00:03:10.065
And do not hesitate to send in
your questions.

00:03:11.108 --> 00:03:15.404
And finally, I will remind you
that this session is recorded

00:03:15.404 --> 00:03:17.072
and will be available on the
CIFAR website

00:03:17.072 --> 00:03:18.407
as soon as next week.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:21.993
Without further ado,
I will give the floor to Yoshua.

00:03:21.993 --> 00:03:25.372
First of all, Yoshua, thank you
so much for joining us.

00:03:25.372 --> 00:03:28.708
And then, I'm going to directly 
tackle the topic

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:30.585
and I think that it's important 
that you start

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:33.922
by clearing up, what is the goal

00:03:33.922 --> 00:03:36.758
of these contact search
applications

00:03:36.758 --> 00:03:40.095
like the one you've developed,
like others have developed

00:03:40.095 --> 00:03:40.762
across the world?

00:03:40.762 --> 00:03:42.139
We'll get back to it, maybe.

00:03:42.514 --> 00:03:44.558
And maybe, because it's
your specialty,

00:03:44.558 --> 00:03:46.309
what is the role of
artificial intelligence

00:03:46.309 --> 00:03:50.939
in such applications,
and does profound learning,

00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:53.233
since you are, just I said,
a specialist,

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:56.570
have a role to play
in these applications?

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:57.612
-Alright.

00:03:57.946 --> 00:03:59.406
Thank you very much, Antoine.

00:03:59.406 --> 00:04:00.907
And thank you for these nice
words.

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:06.455
First of all, the use of phones'
technologies

00:04:06.455 --> 00:04:09.291
to reduce the spread of the
virus.

00:04:09.291 --> 00:04:14.087
The idea is to be able to give 
information

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:17.591
to people who are
maybe contagious

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:20.343
before they infect someone else.

00:04:21.595 --> 00:04:26.433
Because today,
we are facing a virus

00:04:26.433 --> 00:04:28.310
that is very different from
other...

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:31.229
contagious viruses

00:04:31.855 --> 00:04:34.274
and, in particular,
an annoying characteristic

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:37.319
is that people will be
contagious for about two days

00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:40.405
before even having symptoms.

00:04:40.822 --> 00:04:42.741
And, in fact, the peak of
contagiousness

00:04:42.741 --> 00:04:44.075
is before having the symptoms.

00:04:44.409 --> 00:04:46.578
As soon as people start having
symptoms,

00:04:46.578 --> 00:04:49.498
they will start reflecting
on the fact that they might be
contagious

00:04:49.498 --> 00:04:51.708
and, in general,
they change their behavior,

00:04:52.042 --> 00:04:54.544
they will distance themselves,
they will pay more attention,

00:04:56.004 --> 00:04:59.633
and so, a big part of the
infection happens

00:04:59.633 --> 00:05:03.053
at the time where people simply
don't know

00:05:03.512 --> 00:05:04.596
that they are at risk.

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:09.309
So, the different applications
will use

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:13.730
the knowledge of whom met whom

00:05:14.314 --> 00:05:16.191
to be able to share the
information

00:05:16.191 --> 00:05:19.277
between those on whom we have
information,

00:05:19.277 --> 00:05:22.906
the person has symptoms,
the person got a positive
diagnosis,

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:26.660
and those who don't know,
for the moment,
that they are contagious

00:05:27.077 --> 00:05:29.621
but who have been in contact
with these people

00:05:29.621 --> 00:05:32.123
about whom we know
that they are contagious.

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:38.004
In the case of classic
binary screening

00:05:38.755 --> 00:05:40.715
that we find a bit everywhere
in the world,

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:43.927
like in France
and many other countries,

00:05:44.469 --> 00:05:48.390
what we try to do
is to simply detect

00:05:48.723 --> 00:05:52.352
if we are part of
the at-risk category or not,

00:05:52.769 --> 00:05:55.605
in function of the fact
that we were in contact
in the last two weeks

00:05:55.605 --> 00:05:57.399
with someone who had a positive
test

00:05:57.399 --> 00:05:58.733
or a positive diagnosis.

00:06:01.736 --> 00:06:02.612
That, in general,
this diagnosis,

00:06:02.612 --> 00:06:05.532
might come maybe a week

00:06:05.699 --> 00:06:07.659
after the first symptoms.

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:10.579
Because people...

00:06:11.413 --> 00:06:15.500
There isn't necessarily
a sufficient capacity for tests,

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.628
people will wait for their
symptoms to become more serious,

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:21.089
and, in addition,
it takes time

00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:22.382
to do tests.

00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:25.343
It takes time after,
with the manual screening,

00:06:25.594 --> 00:06:29.347
for the people who were in
contact with these positive
people

00:06:29.639 --> 00:06:30.724
receive the information.

00:06:31.016 --> 00:06:33.602
So, the phones allow this
to accelerate a bit more

00:06:34.144 --> 00:06:38.189
and to use the contact screening
that happened

00:06:38.189 --> 00:06:41.860
through the phone,
often with methods that are
secure enough,

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:43.778
in terms of
privacy protection,

00:06:43.778 --> 00:06:45.572
but also not perfect.

00:06:45.572 --> 00:06:47.532
It's impossible to have
perfection in that sense.

00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:50.201
Where artificial intelligence
comes into play,

00:06:50.201 --> 00:06:52.912
is that we can use
other information

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:55.373
that the fact of having
a positive diagnosis

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:59.794
to transmit a signal
to someone with whom we have
been in contact.

00:07:00.211 --> 00:07:02.047
In particular,
if we have symptoms,

00:07:02.422 --> 00:07:05.467
depending on the severity of the
symptoms , the type of symptoms,
etc.

00:07:05.467 --> 00:07:08.803
We have a certain probability
that we are sick.

00:07:08.803 --> 00:07:12.557
And this information,
this probability of being sick,

00:07:12.557 --> 00:07:14.517
can be calculated on our phone

00:07:14.517 --> 00:07:16.895
and be sent
to the phones of people

00:07:16.895 --> 00:07:19.981
who have the application
and with whom we've been in
contact.

00:07:19.981 --> 00:07:23.193
And that, before they themselves
get the symptoms.

00:07:23.193 --> 00:07:25.904
Because there are a few days,
after we've been infected,

00:07:25.904 --> 00:07:29.366
during which
we are not yet infectious.

00:07:30.158 --> 00:07:31.743
And so, if we can send a message

00:07:31.743 --> 00:07:35.830
during this window there,
we can have a big impact

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.207
on the spread of the virus.

00:07:37.499 --> 00:07:39.417
So, it's then that artificial
intelligence takes action

00:07:39.417 --> 00:07:42.504
and it intervenes to predict
the risk in function of all the
hints.

00:07:42.671 --> 00:07:43.546
So, the hints,

00:07:43.546 --> 00:07:44.798
it can be the symptoms,
as I mentioned,

00:07:44.798 --> 00:07:48.468
it can be the agglomeration
of all the risks we've taken

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:50.261
by being close to people

00:07:50.261 --> 00:07:53.139
who themselves had a certain
risk level, etc...

00:07:54.975 --> 00:07:58.061
So, there is the behaviors;
do we take public transports,

00:07:58.061 --> 00:08:02.107
do we put a mask,
do we wash our hands often,
etc...

00:08:02.357 --> 00:08:05.610
that can also come to play
and we can ask these questions
to people

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:07.862
to refine this risk estimation.

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:11.825
-And maybe, to continue in that
direction,

00:08:12.492 --> 00:08:14.619
not everyone is a
profound learning specialist,

00:08:15.036 --> 00:08:16.830
but, to learn,
we need data.

00:08:17.747 --> 00:08:20.959
And finally, as COVID-19
is something new,

00:08:21.418 --> 00:08:25.213
how have you succeeded at having
this data

00:08:25.213 --> 00:08:28.425
that allows to
learning and get a more precis
diagnosis

00:08:28.425 --> 00:08:32.262
than the binary diagnosis
that is sometimes done?

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:33.930
-Very good question.

00:08:34.556 --> 00:08:37.350
So, indeed,
to be able to predict
an event,

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:41.771
like the fact of being
contagious, of being a virus
carrier, etc.,

00:08:42.605 --> 00:08:46.901
there needs to be data
that associates the available
information

00:08:46.901 --> 00:08:48.778
at the moment where we would
want to make a prediction

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.489
and what we want to predict,

00:08:51.948 --> 00:08:53.700
like having the virus, for
example.

00:08:55.285 --> 00:08:57.287
At the beginning, we obviously
don't have this data.

00:08:57.787 --> 00:09:01.791
But we have other data,
epidemiological data,

00:09:03.084 --> 00:09:06.421
coming from epidemiological
models that simulate

00:09:06.755 --> 00:09:09.299
what can happen
in the day-to-day life

00:09:09.299 --> 00:09:13.261
when people walk around,
go from one place to another,

00:09:13.553 --> 00:09:15.346
work, go to school,
go to the hospital,

00:09:15.805 --> 00:09:17.724
spend some time close to each
other.

00:09:19.434 --> 00:09:23.188
And we've built such a model
and colleagues,

00:09:23.188 --> 00:09:24.481
for example, in England,

00:09:24.481 --> 00:09:26.691
have also built similar models

00:09:26.691 --> 00:09:30.820
that allow to test
different communication
strategies

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:32.864
between phones,
information.

00:09:33.364 --> 00:09:34.908
So, with these epidemiological
models,

00:09:34.908 --> 00:09:38.328
we can simulate
what would happen if people,

00:09:38.787 --> 00:09:41.206
by being given a prediction
that concerns them,

00:09:41.498 --> 00:09:43.124
decide to change their behavior

00:09:43.708 --> 00:09:47.420
and how it could affect
the contagions that would happen

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:48.505
or that wouldn't happen.

00:09:49.005 --> 00:09:50.465
So, that, is the first step

00:09:50.673 --> 00:09:54.844
and that would already allow us
to evaluate how different
approaches can be compared

00:09:54.844 --> 00:09:57.555
between each other, from let's
say a theoretical method,
because these models,

00:09:57.555 --> 00:09:59.182
it's not reality,
it's like a physical model,

00:09:59.182 --> 00:10:00.266
it's not reality

00:10:00.266 --> 00:10:04.270
but those are approximations
that are often used
in epidemiology.

00:10:04.896 --> 00:10:06.606
The second thing is,
it's from the moment

00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:08.650
where a system like this
will be expanded,

00:10:09.067 --> 00:10:12.320
we can measure all the
information on the phone

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.698
that serves as entrees
to the prediction.

00:10:16.199 --> 00:10:18.326
And then,
when someone receives a positive
test,

00:10:18.326 --> 00:10:20.245
we can also save this
information

00:10:20.245 --> 00:10:22.038
and it's going to form
a data base

00:10:22.038 --> 00:10:24.374
to train
a pointier predictor

00:10:25.792 --> 00:10:28.419
that doesn't only use
the few dozens of studies

00:10:28.419 --> 00:10:31.256
that give us
a few epidemiological constants

00:10:31.256 --> 00:10:34.342
for simulation,
but something a lot more precis.

00:10:34.801 --> 00:10:38.680
In fact, it would allow getting
data on a scale that,

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:41.683
for now, doesn't exist
in epidemiological studies
of the illness.

00:10:41.683 --> 00:10:43.768
But it would also allow us
to better understand the illness

00:10:43.768 --> 00:10:44.727
and to do better predictions

00:10:44.727 --> 00:10:45.895
of the risk.

00:10:46.312 --> 00:10:47.564
So, there are the privacy
protection issues 

00:10:47.564 --> 00:10:49.899
because it means
that people have to consent,

00:10:49.899 --> 00:10:52.819
if they want, to share this
data

00:10:52.819 --> 00:10:55.155
so that we could train
these risk predictors.

00:10:56.656 --> 00:10:59.826
-This aspect of privacy is
obviously very important.

00:10:59.826 --> 00:11:02.495
As you know,
maybe as the viewers also know,

00:11:02.495 --> 00:11:05.206
that was the subject
of a number of controversies in
France.

00:11:06.207 --> 00:11:10.461
How do we answer the people who
say:

00:11:10.461 --> 00:11:13.673
"Yes, but in the end,
the gains are inferior
to the advantages?"

00:11:13.882 --> 00:11:15.300
Because, then, if I understand
well,

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:16.426
it is not binary.

00:11:16.426 --> 00:11:19.971
I mean, a tracing application,
essentially, is obligated

00:11:19.971 --> 00:11:23.016
to leave some data.

00:11:23.725 --> 00:11:26.561
But, in the end, the pros and
cons need to be weighed.

00:11:27.103 --> 00:11:28.396
So, what do you answer people

00:11:28.396 --> 00:11:30.899
who say that the cons
win against the pros, in the
end?

00:11:31.983 --> 00:11:36.779
-I think we need to have a
serene,

00:11:36.779 --> 00:11:39.908
respectful, and informed
democratic debate

00:11:40.909 --> 00:11:44.287
to decide, together, in every
country,

00:11:44.287 --> 00:11:46.873
and, ideally, on a global scale,

00:11:47.916 --> 00:11:51.461
what weight do we give
these different values.

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:56.841
Is people's lives more or less
important...

00:11:58.259 --> 00:11:59.427
than privacy?

00:11:59.677 --> 00:12:00.762
And it's not all black, all
white,

00:12:00.762 --> 00:12:02.889
because privacy,
it's not that we're losing it
completely.

00:12:02.889 --> 00:12:04.390
For example, the technic that
we've developed

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:08.311
with COVI will protect
the data just as good

00:12:08.311 --> 00:12:10.438
as the way
that the data in a hospital are
protected.

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:13.816
So, we're not talking about
adding a higher risk level

00:12:13.816 --> 00:12:15.109
than what already exists.

00:12:15.443 --> 00:12:19.238
People also use
applications with Bluetooth,
etc.,

00:12:19.238 --> 00:12:22.158
on their phone already,
a lot.

00:12:24.118 --> 00:12:27.705
So, we need to understand these
new risks

00:12:27.705 --> 00:12:30.416
that are added
and understand their advantages.

00:12:31.167 --> 00:12:33.211
At the moment,
to understand the advantages,

00:12:34.295 --> 00:12:36.172
what we have on what is
risk prediction,

00:12:36.172 --> 00:12:37.465
it's only simulations.

00:12:37.465 --> 00:12:39.217
So, we need to do
pilot projects.

00:12:39.217 --> 00:12:40.218
These pilots would need to be

00:12:40.218 --> 00:12:41.636
on a big enough scale

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:43.346
to be statistically valid

00:12:43.721 --> 00:12:46.975
to be able to evaluate, in real
life,

00:12:47.475 --> 00:12:49.310
what degree of...

00:12:49.519 --> 00:12:50.937
how many lives it can save
in the end,

00:12:51.479 --> 00:12:53.898
and so, be able to judge it.

00:12:55.275 --> 00:12:57.360
-Did you also do studies

00:12:57.568 --> 00:12:59.237
during the context of 
simulations on, ultimately,

00:12:59.237 --> 00:13:00.905
what is the percentage of people

00:13:00.905 --> 00:13:02.615
that have to use such an
application

00:13:02.615 --> 00:13:04.701
for it to be efficient?

00:13:05.076 --> 00:13:07.662
Then again, I'm comparing
with France, StopCovid,

00:13:07.662 --> 00:13:11.332
which was launched
nearly a week ago.

00:13:11.749 --> 00:13:13.668
Only 2% 
of the French population

00:13:13.668 --> 00:13:15.253
have downloaded it.

00:13:15.878 --> 00:13:18.840
And we don't even know how many
use it.

00:13:19.674 --> 00:13:22.343
So, there are people that say
that, then, it's not efficient

00:13:22.343 --> 00:13:23.302
and others that say:

00:13:23.302 --> 00:13:24.512
"Yes, it's better than nothing."

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:29.142
So, did you have the time to
study this kind of threshold?

00:13:29.142 --> 00:13:31.519
-There is no simple answer to
this question.

00:13:32.353 --> 00:13:33.980
For example, it depends,

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:37.775
if you have 10% of the
population that use the
application,

00:13:37.775 --> 00:13:40.653
but that this  10% is
concentrated in some places,

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:43.656
well, for these places,
it will be very efficient.

00:13:45.199 --> 00:13:49.871
Then, we have to see what kind
of application we are talking
about.

00:13:50.621 --> 00:13:52.331
So, if it's binary tracing,

00:13:52.331 --> 00:13:53.875
like what is in most countries,

00:13:54.834 --> 00:13:57.378
there are, indeed, studies
that show

00:13:57.378 --> 00:13:59.589
that maybe
60% of the population is needed

00:13:59.589 --> 00:14:00.757
to really have a big impact.

00:14:01.132 --> 00:14:02.341
But with our calculations,

00:14:02.341 --> 00:14:04.427
we show that, sure,
it is not a threshold.

00:14:04.886 --> 00:14:06.054
If it's 50%, it's still useful.

00:14:06.054 --> 00:14:07.263
If it's 40%, it's still useful.

00:14:07.263 --> 00:14:08.389
If it's 30%, it's still useful.

00:14:08.389 --> 00:14:09.891
It's just the use that
decreases.

00:14:11.059 --> 00:14:12.810
So, that is something.

00:14:13.227 --> 00:14:14.896
Another thing
is that an application

00:14:14.896 --> 00:14:16.564
like the one we've built

00:14:18.232 --> 00:14:21.360
stays useful
at a lower level

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:24.030
because it gives information.

00:14:24.363 --> 00:14:26.199
For example,
based on the symptoms,

00:14:26.199 --> 00:14:28.367
it will help us evaluate our own
risk.

00:14:28.367 --> 00:14:31.120
Even if I am in communication
with nobody,

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:34.123
to be able to evaluate the risk
that I am to others

00:14:34.123 --> 00:14:36.167
based on my behaviors,
on my symptoms,

00:14:36.167 --> 00:14:38.002
there are dozens of variations

00:14:38.002 --> 00:14:39.212
on these symptoms,

00:14:39.712 --> 00:14:42.965
well that's something very
useful to help people make their
decisions.

00:14:43.591 --> 00:14:47.303
Second of all, there is an
application like the one we've
put together

00:14:47.303 --> 00:14:50.389
that not only does this
spread of information

00:14:50.389 --> 00:14:51.307
between phones,

00:14:51.307 --> 00:14:54.268
but will also go get data

00:14:54.268 --> 00:14:56.270
to better understand the
illness,

00:14:57.313 --> 00:14:59.857
to help public health in
identifying

00:14:59.857 --> 00:15:03.611
where the outbreaks to come are,

00:15:04.028 --> 00:15:07.740
in a way, to be able to
anticipate these outbreaks,

00:15:10.118 --> 00:15:11.702
it is useful

00:15:11.702 --> 00:15:13.955
even if the percentage
of the population is low

00:15:13.955 --> 00:15:15.915
because, at that moment,
we will go

00:15:15.915 --> 00:15:18.501
look at the aggregated data
on many people

00:15:18.501 --> 00:15:20.169
or on an area.

00:15:20.586 --> 00:15:23.589
We don't need everyone
in the area to use the
application.

00:15:24.132 --> 00:15:26.759
If 10% of the people in
the area use the application,

00:15:26.759 --> 00:15:28.511
we're still going to have a
signal

00:15:28.511 --> 00:15:30.680
that will be useful
for public health.

00:15:32.056 --> 00:15:35.226
-And if I place myself
on a technical point of view,

00:15:35.226 --> 00:15:37.103
the communications
between phones,

00:15:37.353 --> 00:15:38.896
it's on Bluetooth technologies?

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:40.481
(Yoshua Bengio)
Yes.

00:15:40.815 --> 00:15:42.316
-And then again, in France,
there was,

00:15:42.316 --> 00:15:43.985
and I assume it's the same for
where you live,

00:15:43.985 --> 00:15:46.904
a controversy around Apple,
not to hide,

00:15:46.904 --> 00:15:50.950
that considered that it blocked
in a certain way,

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:55.788
the Bluetooth connection
to this type of application.

00:15:55.788 --> 00:15:59.208
How have you planned to solve
this problem?

00:16:00.459 --> 00:16:02.670
-There are many solutions
possible.

00:16:05.173 --> 00:16:08.801
One solution
is that we asked

00:16:08.801 --> 00:16:11.679
Apple and Google to change their
protocol

00:16:12.096 --> 00:16:14.432
in a manner that we find minor,

00:16:14.557 --> 00:16:15.766
that doesn't touch
the Bluetooth,

00:16:15.766 --> 00:16:20.021
but simply at a consent issue.

00:16:21.314 --> 00:16:22.857
I don't really have the time to
dive

00:16:22.857 --> 00:16:25.026
into the details,
but it is, according to us,

00:16:25.026 --> 00:16:29.447
a really small change
that would allow, if they do it,

00:16:29.447 --> 00:16:33.242
to roll predictive models
with their interface.

00:16:35.286 --> 00:16:37.872
In the meantime,
or as an alternative,

00:16:38.998 --> 00:16:43.794
we've developed a
detection of
Bluetooth proximity,

00:16:43.794 --> 00:16:45.963
based on the work that's been
done in England

00:16:45.963 --> 00:16:50.509
by the NHSX and that was tested
in the Isle of Wight

00:16:50.509 --> 00:16:53.971
and that seems to give really
good results.

00:16:54.055 --> 00:16:56.390
And, in fact, even has a better
phone coverage

00:16:57.058 --> 00:16:59.018
than we would have with Apple
and Google.

00:16:59.018 --> 00:17:01.938
In the sense that,
with Apple and Google,

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:05.233
there are older phones

00:17:05.233 --> 00:17:06.901
for which it obviously wouldn't
work

00:17:06.901 --> 00:17:09.237
because they wouldn't be able
to update the operating system.

00:17:09.862 --> 00:17:11.280
-I'm reminding viewers that they
can ask

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:14.158
questions and I'll be their
spokesperson.

00:17:14.533 --> 00:17:16.202
There is still a subject
we need to tackle.

00:17:16.911 --> 00:17:18.204
I follow this from afar

00:17:18.204 --> 00:17:20.039
because Paris-Toronto,
Paris-Montreal

00:17:20.039 --> 00:17:21.540
it's not exactly
side-by-side.

00:17:21.958 --> 00:17:23.292
But I understood that,
in the end,

00:17:23.292 --> 00:17:26.379
the federal government had
decided not to keep COVI.

00:17:26.629 --> 00:17:29.465
So, I imagine that it was a
deception for all the people

00:17:29.465 --> 00:17:31.634
who must have spent a lot of
hours

00:17:31.634 --> 00:17:32.885
and, probably many nights.

00:17:33.511 --> 00:17:35.805
Can you still comment on this
decision?

00:17:36.347 --> 00:17:39.016
-I think that the role of
researchers

00:17:40.268 --> 00:17:44.564
is to offer solutions with their
science

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:48.818
with what is, in reality
art in technology and in
science.

00:17:50.361 --> 00:17:52.321
It is not the role of
researchers

00:17:52.321 --> 00:17:54.615
to make decisions for the
population.

00:17:55.825 --> 00:17:57.910
The government represents the
population,

00:17:57.910 --> 00:18:00.329
they made this choice in Canada.

00:18:01.789 --> 00:18:03.165
I think they have their reasons.

00:18:03.708 --> 00:18:06.627
I can understand
that

00:18:07.503 --> 00:18:09.964
it's easier to explain

00:18:10.172 --> 00:18:12.675
that we're going to use the same
kind of applications as the ones

00:18:12.925 --> 00:18:14.260
we do in the United States,
for example,

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:17.596
in many other places.

00:18:17.596 --> 00:18:19.140
And there's a fear,
maybe,

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:20.891
that the fact of using
artificial intelligence,

00:18:20.891 --> 00:18:22.393
it pushes people away.

00:18:23.728 --> 00:18:25.605
So, there are arguments that can
hold.

00:18:26.022 --> 00:18:29.066
For my part, the fact of being
able to save lives,

00:18:29.692 --> 00:18:32.320
and to help the public health
with more precise information

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:33.863
to fight the virus,

00:18:34.488 --> 00:18:37.325
it should've won the round

00:18:37.325 --> 00:18:39.577
but it's not up to us to make
these decisions.

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:43.289
-Of course, it's important,
actually,

00:18:43.289 --> 00:18:46.042
as you say,
that we do not get mixed up.

00:18:46.042 --> 00:18:47.626
Politics are here to decide,

00:18:47.626 --> 00:18:49.837
and scientists,
to bring expertise.

00:18:49.837 --> 00:18:51.172
But I think that you're
absolutely right

00:18:51.172 --> 00:18:53.924
on the fact that it's not up to
the scientists to decide,

00:18:53.924 --> 00:18:55.051
that would be a confusion of
types.

00:18:55.301 --> 00:18:59.347
According to you, even though,
I imagine, it's not really a
simple question

00:18:59.347 --> 00:19:02.183
but, in the government's
decision,

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:06.854
what was the part linked to
whether Canadians were ready or
not

00:19:06.854 --> 00:19:09.523
to accept this kind of
application?

00:19:09.982 --> 00:19:11.859
Then again, if I compare to
France,

00:19:12.151 --> 00:19:15.446
there was a lot of controversy
and a lot of forums,
so, the for the against,

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:17.656
as we really love discussions in
France,

00:19:17.656 --> 00:19:18.699
as you know.

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:23.079
It played a role, do you think,
or is it more a technical
decision?

00:19:23.829 --> 00:19:26.082
-No, I think it's really a
political decision.

00:19:26.582 --> 00:19:30.836
Actually, the fear of putting
something forward

00:19:31.253 --> 00:19:35.216
that is then not pursued by
enough people.

00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:37.510
So, we see, still, for example,

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:40.179
that in Alberta, where there's
an application that was
launched,

00:19:40.721 --> 00:19:41.847
there is about 5% of the
population

00:19:41.847 --> 00:19:43.641
that downloaded the application.

00:19:46.769 --> 00:19:48.896
So, I think there's an issue

00:19:50.564 --> 00:19:54.443
of succeeding in a political
move that is important,

00:19:55.194 --> 00:19:56.278
that can be understood.

00:19:57.571 --> 00:19:59.824
-And now, we finally come back,

00:19:59.824 --> 00:20:01.117
you've worked a lot,

00:20:01.117 --> 00:20:03.285
all your team and many people
around you,

00:20:03.744 --> 00:20:06.997
what were, in the end,
from a scientific point of view,

00:20:07.707 --> 00:20:09.875
was it just an engineering work

00:20:10.501 --> 00:20:13.170
or, rather, what were
the scientific obstacles

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:15.589
that caused you the most
problems.

00:20:15.965 --> 00:20:17.633
-Ah, I really like this question

00:20:17.633 --> 00:20:20.636
because it's an example
of the type

00:20:21.303 --> 00:20:22.763
we like to see

00:20:24.390 --> 00:20:26.350
where, at the same time, there's
an application

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:28.936
that is interesting
and useful for the public,

00:20:28.936 --> 00:20:30.146
for society,

00:20:30.646 --> 00:20:33.649
but, while
causing scientific challenges
unheard of.

00:20:34.233 --> 00:20:36.402
So then, unheard of
scientific challenges,

00:20:36.402 --> 00:20:37.570
at the artificial intelligence
level,

00:20:37.570 --> 00:20:39.196
at the automatic learning level,

00:20:39.655 --> 00:20:43.159
was to work
with the privacy constraints.

00:20:44.118 --> 00:20:47.580
Example, if we really wanted to
be optimal

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:50.332
in the risk prediction,
we would need to have access

00:20:51.542 --> 00:20:52.835
to all the contacts collection.

00:20:53.127 --> 00:20:57.173
Who was in contact with whom,
when, for how long,
etc.

00:20:57.882 --> 00:20:59.300
With the graph of all these
contacts.

00:20:59.717 --> 00:21:02.636
But, that
is privileged information,

00:21:03.137 --> 00:21:04.472
too dangerous.

00:21:05.181 --> 00:21:07.516
We don't want that any person,

00:21:07.725 --> 00:21:10.144
that any government can have
access

00:21:10.144 --> 00:21:10.978
to this information.

00:21:10.978 --> 00:21:12.188
So, in fact, this information

00:21:12.188 --> 00:21:13.939
is implicit
in the phone networks,

00:21:14.148 --> 00:21:15.149
but it's never exposed

00:21:15.149 --> 00:21:16.358
anywhere with our system.

00:21:17.526 --> 00:21:21.739
Nobody can know
who met who, where, when.

00:21:26.076 --> 00:21:29.789
So, we had to innovate
at the algorithms level

00:21:29.789 --> 00:21:32.041
to infer this level of risk

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:34.210
with missing information.

00:21:34.919 --> 00:21:37.713
And others, in the same kind,

00:21:38.005 --> 00:21:40.799
where, in the end, we don't have
everything needed

00:21:41.091 --> 00:21:42.635
to make a prediction,

00:21:42.635 --> 00:21:45.346
but how we can come out of it
from a technical point of view,

00:21:45.346 --> 00:21:46.889
at the level of learning
algorithms,

00:21:47.264 --> 00:21:49.517
to still succeed in making
predictions

00:21:49.517 --> 00:21:51.560
and train the system
without having

00:21:52.561 --> 00:21:55.397
the real answer.

00:21:57.566 --> 00:22:01.487
-We're starting to have many
questions from the audience.

00:22:02.029 --> 00:22:04.281
And one of the questions a
little pessimist, I would like
to say

00:22:05.699 --> 00:22:07.993
that is asked, is "Are you ready
for the second wave?"

00:22:09.203 --> 00:22:10.663
If, unfortunately, I add,

00:22:10.663 --> 00:22:13.666
we had that second wave in the
fall,

00:22:13.666 --> 00:22:14.959
like some fear.

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:18.921
-Us, our technology is ready.

00:22:19.505 --> 00:22:22.174
We're going to put our code
as open source

00:22:22.174 --> 00:22:23.175
pretty fast.

00:22:24.552 --> 00:22:25.845
And so...

00:22:26.095 --> 00:22:28.722
I think that after...

00:22:29.139 --> 00:22:31.433
it's a public debate

00:22:31.433 --> 00:22:34.061
that has to continue.

00:22:34.061 --> 00:22:36.355
With what is put in place
nowadays,

00:22:36.355 --> 00:22:38.649
with the tracing, that I kind of
call binary

00:22:38.649 --> 00:22:40.693
will not work well in some
countries,

00:22:40.693 --> 00:22:42.820
and there's a second wave
that's coming,

00:22:43.195 --> 00:22:46.240
maybe people will tell
themselves: "Can we do better?"

00:22:46.448 --> 00:22:47.866
At that moment, the articifial
intelligence use

00:22:47.866 --> 00:22:50.869
will maybe become something

00:22:50.869 --> 00:22:52.162
that will be considered more
seriously.

00:22:53.372 --> 00:22:55.457
-There is certainly in the
audience,

00:22:55.457 --> 00:22:57.126
people who are supportive,
if I can say,

00:22:57.668 --> 00:22:58.961
and who ask, in a certain way,

00:22:58.961 --> 00:23:02.423
can the citizen or citizens

00:23:02.423 --> 00:23:04.967
help convince the politics?

00:23:05.384 --> 00:23:07.428
For example, there is a
consultation

00:23:07.928 --> 00:23:10.347
that maybe we should review the
decision

00:23:11.307 --> 00:23:13.851
and reimagine the use
of COVI at a higher scale.

00:23:15.603 --> 00:23:17.313
-I greatly hope,
in fact,

00:23:17.646 --> 00:23:20.399
that we consider creating this
kind of consultation.

00:23:22.192 --> 00:23:24.236
Obviously, there will be
surveys, eventually,

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:27.156
at the discretion of discussions
in the media,

00:23:27.156 --> 00:23:28.991
in public places.

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:31.994
But us, among our values,

00:23:32.912 --> 00:23:37.333
there is the question of
giving power to the citizens.

00:23:37.333 --> 00:23:39.043
So, in reality,
this kind of application,

00:23:39.376 --> 00:23:41.587
if they are well designed
at the privacy protection level,

00:23:42.796 --> 00:23:47.384
they are democratic tools
where it's not the government 

00:23:47.718 --> 00:23:49.303
that decides for us.

00:23:50.429 --> 00:23:53.974
It's each citizen that decides
to do a bit of his share,

00:23:53.974 --> 00:23:56.935
from a civic point of view,
to protect his community.

00:23:58.062 --> 00:24:02.942
And, it also means,
be part of discussions,
democratic ones, public ones,

00:24:03.651 --> 00:24:05.611
on the pros and cons,
and the issues.

00:24:05.861 --> 00:24:07.863
And I hope we're going to have
more of these discussions

00:24:07.863 --> 00:24:09.615
in the future and so, actually,
I obviously welcome

00:24:09.615 --> 00:24:11.200
today's discussion as such.

00:24:12.826 --> 00:24:15.162
-Maybe then,
since we only have five minutes
left,

00:24:15.162 --> 00:24:16.038
and time passes fast,
does...

00:24:16.038 --> 00:24:19.208
I remember, at the very
beginning, that you are still

00:24:19.208 --> 00:24:21.627
the big specialist
of profound learning

00:24:21.752 --> 00:24:24.296
and one of the big shots of AI,

00:24:24.296 --> 00:24:26.382
does artificial intelligence,

00:24:26.382 --> 00:24:27.633
and maybe profound learning,

00:24:27.966 --> 00:24:31.011
beyond the tracing applications
which we've spoken a lot about,

00:24:31.428 --> 00:24:35.182
were useful and used during the
pandemic?

00:24:35.975 --> 00:24:39.645
-Ah, yes, there are many things
that are happening.

00:24:41.689 --> 00:24:43.857
At the start, there have already
been many articles

00:24:43.857 --> 00:24:46.026
that have come out on the study,
for example,

00:24:46.026 --> 00:24:50.406
of medical images
to help diagnose

00:24:50.406 --> 00:24:52.700
before the tests are even done.

00:24:53.492 --> 00:24:56.578
There are ongoing studies to
better understand

00:24:57.037 --> 00:24:58.205
the patients' risk.

00:24:58.205 --> 00:24:59.498
So, not with the phone,

00:24:59.498 --> 00:25:01.208
but from what's happening in the
hospital.

00:25:01.667 --> 00:25:04.878
Which are the patients
that we need to take care of

00:25:04.878 --> 00:25:06.171
in priority, for example,

00:25:06.171 --> 00:25:09.550
or that we have to put
in an artificial respirator,
etc.

00:25:10.801 --> 00:25:13.637
I, myself, got involved
in other projects

00:25:14.138 --> 00:25:16.682
where we use profound learning
for the discovery

00:25:16.682 --> 00:25:20.352
of new medicine,
or new medicine combinations.

00:25:21.145 --> 00:25:24.189
So then, we use automatic
learning,

00:25:24.189 --> 00:25:28.110
profound learning,
to research faster and more
efficiently

00:25:28.527 --> 00:25:30.404
in the molecular field

00:25:31.947 --> 00:25:34.616
that is the most promising
to the pass

00:25:34.616 --> 00:25:37.661
these candidates to
biologists that will test them.

00:25:39.413 --> 00:25:41.749
The set of combinations,
either of existing medicines

00:25:41.749 --> 00:25:43.792
or new molecules altogether,

00:25:44.168 --> 00:25:47.171
is too, too big
to be able to all test them

00:25:47.171 --> 00:25:48.505
or even test a fraction.

00:25:50.048 --> 00:25:52.509
-And in that field,
are there promising primary
results?

00:25:54.011 --> 00:25:58.307
-There have been studies
that have identified

00:25:58.807 --> 00:26:01.769
potential medications
at the individual level,
already,

00:26:01.769 --> 00:26:06.482
and that was done,
and I think that it probably
influenced

00:26:06.482 --> 00:26:07.775
the clinical studies in
progress.

00:26:08.233 --> 00:26:09.526
Where we are today,

00:26:09.526 --> 00:26:11.862
it is the combination of two
medications.

00:26:12.237 --> 00:26:14.531
So then, I don't think the
results have come out yet,

00:26:14.531 --> 00:26:15.949
but it will come in the next few
weeks,

00:26:15.949 --> 00:26:17.034
the next few months.

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:20.704
Also, literally,
new medications.

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:24.750
What is interesting
with the medication
combinations

00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:26.960
is that it's going to be faster
to unfold

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:30.172
because we don't need to test
if these molecules are
dangerous.

00:26:30.172 --> 00:26:32.341
We already know what the side
effects are.

00:26:33.383 --> 00:26:35.469
So, we only need to really
verify

00:26:35.469 --> 00:26:39.598
that the combination of medicine
helps fight the illness.

00:26:40.474 --> 00:26:43.101
It needs to be understood
that these treatments,

00:26:43.101 --> 00:26:46.438
in fact, is also a last resort,
we can say

00:26:46.438 --> 00:26:47.481
before the vaccine.

00:26:48.106 --> 00:26:50.651
But a vaccine, there is a lot of
development

00:26:50.651 --> 00:26:51.735
surrounding that.

00:26:51.735 --> 00:26:53.821
But it's going to be long,
because, well,

00:26:53.821 --> 00:26:55.864
maybe we won't find what
functions fast enough.

00:26:55.864 --> 00:26:57.491
But even if we find something
that functions,

00:26:58.116 --> 00:27:01.036
it really needs to be tested
thoroughly

00:27:01.036 --> 00:27:05.249
because we cannot allow
ourselves

00:27:05.249 --> 00:27:08.669
to give a molecule
to 99% of the population

00:27:08.669 --> 00:27:10.295
and then realize six months
later,

00:27:10.295 --> 00:27:11.922
that half of the people are
dying from it.

00:27:12.756 --> 00:27:13.757
-That would be a problem.

00:27:13.757 --> 00:27:16.760
We had a few controversies
in France, about this subject,

00:27:16.760 --> 00:27:18.387
on certain molecules,

00:27:18.804 --> 00:27:20.889
and I think that everyone
hears well what you're saying.

00:27:20.889 --> 00:27:23.350
-Whereas antivirals,
we give them

00:27:23.350 --> 00:27:24.810
only to people who are already
sick, in general.

00:27:25.352 --> 00:27:26.186
-Of course.

00:27:26.436 --> 00:27:29.314
And these questions,
the definition of new
molecules...

00:27:30.274 --> 00:27:32.359
I think that defining
new material goes a little

00:27:32.359 --> 00:27:33.694
in the same type of research,

00:27:34.361 --> 00:27:37.155
does that present,
for the AI specialists like you,

00:27:37.155 --> 00:27:38.866
new questions?

00:27:39.658 --> 00:27:41.243
-Ah yes, absolutely!

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:45.789
It's an extraordinary source of
questions that are not evident.

00:27:46.748 --> 00:27:49.793
For example, one of the
questions that interest me

00:27:50.335 --> 00:27:52.671
is how to use
learning by reinforcement

00:27:54.882 --> 00:27:56.925
to have a strategy research

00:27:56.925 --> 00:28:00.137
in the medication field
that takes into account

00:28:00.137 --> 00:28:01.388
the particularities
of that problem

00:28:02.389 --> 00:28:04.016
and that is different from the
use

00:28:04.016 --> 00:28:05.350
of learning by reinforcement

00:28:05.976 --> 00:28:06.977
in the applications that we know

00:28:06.977 --> 00:28:10.606
like playing the Go game,
chess or Atari.

00:28:12.774 --> 00:28:14.484
And it causes new problems that
we don't know

00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:18.447
at the manner of exploring this
field level,

00:28:18.447 --> 00:28:20.908
to use the fact that there is an
issue of time to calculate.

00:28:21.366 --> 00:28:23.285
In fact, we are in a kind of
race against the watch,

00:28:23.368 --> 00:28:25.454
we have a limited calculation
power,

00:28:25.454 --> 00:28:28.707
how we use this calculation
power optimally,

00:28:28.707 --> 00:28:31.293
to extract the more information

00:28:31.293 --> 00:28:32.294
on the possible candidates.

00:28:33.462 --> 00:28:34.212
-Listen, Yoshua,

00:28:34.212 --> 00:28:36.006
we have a minute left,
we have to wrap
this up.

00:28:36.006 --> 00:28:37.674
There are many recurring
questions

00:28:37.674 --> 00:28:40.427
about the COVI application
and the tracing applications.

00:28:41.678 --> 00:28:46.475
With questions on how
we can convince people,

00:28:47.684 --> 00:28:49.728
isn't the application less
bad...

00:28:49.937 --> 00:28:51.563
It relates to the question we
already had,

00:28:52.648 --> 00:28:54.441
isn't what the application is
bringing less bad than 

00:28:54.441 --> 00:28:56.360
what it takes away in terms of
freedom?

00:28:56.944 --> 00:28:58.320
So, there you go,
you have one minute

00:28:58.320 --> 00:29:00.364
to convince people
that next time,

00:29:00.364 --> 00:29:02.574
they have to vote for,
if I can say.

00:29:03.492 --> 00:29:05.285
-I'm going to take a very simple
example

00:29:05.702 --> 00:29:07.329
that isn't covered by

00:29:08.455 --> 00:29:10.666
the actual tracing systems.

00:29:11.458 --> 00:29:12.876
Let's imagine that...

00:29:13.335 --> 00:29:14.461
Antoine...

00:29:15.712 --> 00:29:18.715
has COVID symptoms,

00:29:19.675 --> 00:29:21.843
and that Antoine and I
have seen each other,

00:29:21.843 --> 00:29:22.928
three days ago.

00:29:24.346 --> 00:29:25.555
Three days ago,
I got infected

00:29:25.555 --> 00:29:27.140
but I don't know it yet.

00:29:29.643 --> 00:29:32.020
I start,
three days later,

00:29:32.854 --> 00:29:34.564
at the moment where Antoine
starts having symptoms,

00:29:34.815 --> 00:29:36.733
I start being contagious.

00:29:37.609 --> 00:29:39.194
If it was possible

00:29:39.486 --> 00:29:40.654
that I get warned

00:29:40.904 --> 00:29:42.072
that there's a chance that I am,

00:29:42.072 --> 00:29:44.992
because Antoine announced he has
symptoms,

00:29:45.534 --> 00:29:47.411
we could save many lives...

00:29:47.869 --> 00:29:48.745
and...

00:29:49.413 --> 00:29:52.374
we can do it by not taking
any more risks

00:29:52.374 --> 00:29:54.334
with our privacy than we already
do.

00:29:55.210 --> 00:29:56.253
-Listen, Yoshua,
thank you so much.

00:29:56.253 --> 00:29:57.921
All you have left to do is
organize

00:29:57.921 --> 00:30:00.340
consultations for the next wave,

00:30:00.340 --> 00:30:01.216
even though we all hope

00:30:01.216 --> 00:30:02.551
that there will not be a second
wave.

00:30:03.176 --> 00:30:05.095
Thank you to all the
participants,

00:30:05.345 --> 00:30:07.472
there was over fifty
participants,

00:30:07.723 --> 00:30:09.057
so I think we had a good
audience.

00:30:10.434 --> 00:30:12.310
Thank you, thank you again
Yoshua.

00:30:12.310 --> 00:30:13.562
Thank you everyone.

00:30:14.146 --> 00:30:16.690
Until next time, for a new
conference of CIFAR.

00:30:17.149 --> 00:30:18.525
-Thank you very much, Antoine.

00:30:18.692 --> 00:30:19.693
-Until next time!

